## Actions — tone (reviewer findings, verify in place)

1. Drop assistant tics: "Notably," opener (~L862); "That is precisely
   what" (~L921); "Let us admit it:" oratory (~L196-224 GEM paragraph)
   → plain statement.
2. De-jargon the system subsection §7.4 (~L913-946), worst cluster:
   "stateful pipeline" echoes (×5 "pipeline" in ~20 lines — keep the
   term once, then "such a system"/"that investment"); "sharpens the
   target"; "derived projection" → "snapshot drawn from the narrative
   inventory at a reference date"; develop-or-trim the two-sentence
   knowledge-graph-triple analogy (give it its one-clause payoff,
   drop "therefore the same problem as" identity overclaim).
3. De-jargon Future research (~L1155-1185): spell out "bitemporal claim
   log" for a statistics/energy reader ("each claim records both when
   the fact held and when it was asserted"); drop "modality layer" and
   "fold into the actual state" vocabulary; "arbitrate" → "settle";
   future-system assertions in present tense → proposal mood ("This
   suggests extracting structure once at ingest...").
4. Discussion: "Phase C cross-evaluation" used cold (~L1051) → gloss or
   drop; "for three compounding reasons" → plainer; "Read together, the
   three literatures locate the continuation" meta-sentence (~L1128) →
   delete, start with the content.
5. "pipeline" outside architecture sections (~L204 "automated pipeline"
   → "automated method"); "reproduced verbatim" after "verbatim
   prompts" (~L459-461) → single verbatim.
6. Em-dash-dense sentences flagged (~L1008-1013 interday drift, 3
   dashes): restructure into two sentences, NET dash reduction.
7. Sweep the rest of the body for the same classes (the list above is
   seed, not bound): empty intensifiers, agent hedge-speak ("The value
   may therefore lie in"), CS idiom in prose.

